1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,396
Listen closely… That’s the sound of a heart that has never needed a battery.

2
00:00:05,646 --> 00:00:08,507
Four beats every second.

3
00:00:08,557 --> 00:00:09,879
Every hour.

4
00:00:10,129 --> 00:00:11,574
Every day.

5
00:00:11,824 --> 00:00:14,138
For as long as you wear it.

6
00:00:16,088 --> 00:00:19,800
This is calibre 3235.

7
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:26,722
It beats inside some of Rolex’s best-loved watches: the Datejust, the Submariner Date, and the Sea-Dweller.

8
00:00:26,972 --> 00:00:29,855
From the outside, it’s calm.

9
00:00:29,905 --> 00:00:31,327
Composed.

10
00:00:31,577 --> 00:00:35,710
Just a sweeping seconds hand, and a date at three o’clock.

11
00:00:36,260 --> 00:00:38,166
But turn it over…

12
00:00:39,716 --> 00:00:43,652
Open it up, and you’ll find something rather wonderful.

13
00:00:47,402 --> 00:00:48,966
No battery.

14
00:00:49,016 --> 00:00:50,905
No circuitry.

15
00:00:50,955 --> 00:00:55,928
Just springs, wheels and jewels, every one made in-house, and every one with a job to do.

16
00:00:56,178 --> 00:01:02,221
So let’s take it apart together, one layer at a time, and follow the energy as it flows.

17
00:01:04,871 --> 00:01:10,854
Lift the layers apart, and the movement becomes a stack… a little tower of engineering.

18
00:01:11,104 --> 00:01:13,833
The winding rotor on top.

19
00:01:13,883 --> 00:01:21,174
Then the bridges, set with 31 synthetic rubies: tiny, hard-wearing bearings that keep friction to a whisper.

20
00:01:21,424 --> 00:01:27,078
Beneath them, the balance, the escapement, the gear train, the mainspring barrel and the main plate.

21
00:01:27,128 --> 00:01:31,945
And at the very bottom, on the dial side, the parts that move the hands and turn the date.

22
00:01:33,945 --> 00:01:37,343
We'll start at the top, with the Perpetual rotor.

23
00:01:37,393 --> 00:01:44,707
An idea Rolex patented in nineteen thirty-one… and still, I think, one of the most elegant in all of watchmaking.

24
00:01:44,957 --> 00:01:49,438
It’s a half-moon of dense metal, spinning freely on a ball bearing.

25
00:01:49,488 --> 00:01:53,011
Every time your wrist moves, it moves.

26
00:01:53,261 --> 00:02:00,002
A pair of reversing wheels takes that swing, whichever way it goes, and turns it into one steady winding force.

27
00:02:00,252 --> 00:02:04,061
So simply by wearing it, you’re keeping it alive.

28
00:02:06,061 --> 00:02:09,727
All that winding flows here, into the mainspring.

29
00:02:09,777 --> 00:02:14,235
A long ribbon of alloy, coiled inside this drum we call the barrel.

30
00:02:14,485 --> 00:02:21,286
Rolex gave the barrel thinner walls, which leaves room for a longer spring, without making the barrel one bit bigger.

31
00:02:21,536 --> 00:02:25,329
Fully wound, it holds around 70 hours of power.

32
00:02:25,379 --> 00:02:30,181
Take it off on Friday evening, and come Monday morning, it’s still running.

33
00:02:30,431 --> 00:02:33,690
The spring doesn’t let go all at once.

34
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:38,534
It gives that energy up slowly, patiently, through the wheels below.

35
00:02:40,534 --> 00:02:42,782
This is the gear train.

36
00:02:42,832 --> 00:02:48,291
Each large wheel drives the small pinion of the next, and with every step, the speed rises.

37
00:02:48,541 --> 00:02:53,007
The centre wheel turns once an hour, and carries the minute hand.

38
00:02:53,257 --> 00:02:57,625
The fourth wheel turns once a minute, and drives the seconds hand.

39
00:02:57,875 --> 00:03:02,937
But left to itself, this train would simply race until the spring ran flat.

40
00:03:02,987 --> 00:03:05,849
So something has to hold it back.

41
00:03:07,849 --> 00:03:10,780
And that something is the escapement.

42
00:03:10,830 --> 00:03:15,053
Here it is, slowed right down, so we can really see it.

43
00:03:15,303 --> 00:03:18,162
The escape wheel wants to turn.

44
00:03:18,212 --> 00:03:25,370
The pallet fork catches it, lets it slip forward by half a tooth, and in return, gives the balance a gentle push.

45
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:26,837
Lock.

46
00:03:27,087 --> 00:03:28,422
Release.

47
00:03:28,672 --> 00:03:29,974
Impulse.

48
00:03:30,224 --> 00:03:32,869
That’s the tick you hear.

49
00:03:33,119 --> 00:03:39,590
Rolex’s own Chronergy escapement is around 15 per cent more efficient than a conventional lever design.

50
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:45,331
And because it’s made of nickel-phosphorus, magnetic fields simply can’t throw it off.

51
00:03:47,331 --> 00:03:50,465
And here is the heart of the watch.

52
00:03:50,515 --> 00:03:53,601
The balance wheel, and its hairspring.

53
00:03:53,851 --> 00:03:57,595
It swings to and fro four times every second.

54
00:03:57,645 --> 00:04:01,450
That’s 28,800 beats an hour.

55
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:02,822
Every hour.

56
00:04:03,072 --> 00:04:04,517
Every day.

57
00:04:04,767 --> 00:04:08,835
The blue Parachrom hairspring is made from niobium and zirconium.

58
00:04:08,885 --> 00:04:17,156
It shrugs off magnetism, it’s unbothered by heat and cold, and after a knock it’s up to ten times more precise than a conventional spring.

59
00:04:17,406 --> 00:04:23,273
And Rolex’s Paraflex shock absorbers stand guard over its delicate pivots.

60
00:04:26,123 --> 00:04:29,393
Now, let’s turn it over, to the dial side.

61
00:04:29,643 --> 00:04:37,129
Here, a little set of gears called the motion works turns the hour hand at exactly one-twelfth the speed of the minute hand.

62
00:04:37,379 --> 00:04:40,168
And at midnight… watch the date.

63
00:04:40,218 --> 00:04:41,964
It doesn't creep.

64
00:04:42,014 --> 00:04:43,344
It jumps!

65
00:04:43,594 --> 00:04:45,189
Instantly.

66
00:04:47,439 --> 00:04:51,331
Put it all back together, and every part plays its role.

67
00:04:51,381 --> 00:04:53,377
The rotor winds.

68
00:04:53,427 --> 00:04:55,344
The spring stores.

69
00:04:55,394 --> 00:04:57,402
The wheels count.

70
00:04:57,452 --> 00:05:00,087
The escapement releases.

71
00:05:00,137 --> 00:05:02,973
And the balance keeps time.

72
00:05:03,223 --> 00:05:08,805
Rolex tests every watch once it’s cased, to within two seconds a day, fast or slow.

73
00:05:08,855 --> 00:05:12,614
They call it the Superlative Chronometer standard.

74
00:05:12,864 --> 00:05:16,871
And it does all of that without ever needing a battery.

75
00:05:16,921 --> 00:05:20,445
Now that is beautiful engineering.

